  "_comment": "Shared Rust<->TS parity fixture (ITEM-7). The Rust test (src/modules/datatype/tests/mod.rs) and the FE e2e (ui/tests/e2e/datatype/registry.spec.ts) both read THIS file and assert their registry computes every case identically. One source of truth for is_a/accepts/viewers_for/layout_kind behavior. WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND MUST NOT COME BACK: a `lattice` array enumerating every type in the registry (it held 26 entries) plus an exact `reg.len()` count. That made this fixture an ENUMERATION of a set the package tree contributes to, so contributing a datatype forced an edit to a file under src-app/server/src/ -- mutually unsatisfiable with the zero-core invariant the package system rests on. The full type set is now DERIVED in mod.rs from the Rust roots UNION every contributes.datatypes[].id in every src-app/packages/*/manifest.yaml, which is a strictly stronger check and needs no edit when a package is added. See docs/design/derived-guards.md section 4; `the_shared_golden_holds_case_tables_and_no_frozen_lattice` fails if `lattice` returns. The four tables below are enumerated CASES over the lattice, not an enumeration OF it, so a new datatype does not invalidate them and they stay. ROUND-1 AUDIT ADDITION: twelve `is_a` rows were added for `annotation-table`, `cell-set`, `de/result`, `geneset/collection` and `graph/attributed` \u2014 the five contributed types that, once `lattice` was deleted, no case table mentioned at all, leaving their declared `extends` frozen nowhere. Each row pins one parent claim (and one NON-parent, so a re-root to a sibling is caught too). This is a ONE-TIME addition for types that already exist: contributing a datatype tomorrow still requires no edit to this file, which is the property INV-3 asserts. Read the derivation and these tables as two halves \u2014 the derivation cross-checks declared-vs-loaded and is edit-free; the tables freeze VALUES and cost an edit per covered type.",
